  "answer_core": "Do not choose a dissertation direction right now. Treat the next few weeks as a short, low‑cost test of whether the Silva/data path is actually real and whether your existing fallback can still carry a dissertation if the data path fails.\n\nUse two gates. Gate 1: the Silva/data gate. Confirm what data exists, who controls it, when you can inspect it, what the collaboration terms and authorship expectations are, and whether someone can cover the single‑cell gaps. Only proceed if you get concrete access on a clear timeline and your advisor explicitly supports the move. Gate 2: the fallback gate. Verify that your current direction remains advisor‑backed, resourced, technically ready, and available for you to re‑enter. Set a clear stop‑date for the Silva probe and define visible conditions for saying no.\n\nKeep the political sequence clean. Talk to your advisor first. Use Silva only for concrete checks on data availability and timing. Go to the committee only after you know whether the data path is real and whether your advisor would support a shift without pulling you back toward the safer lab path.\n\nRun the Silva probe strictly within the window you set. Make the dissertation decision only after both gates have evidence, not vibes. Choose the direction that can actually become a finishable PhD, not the one that merely feels exciting.",
